<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Riga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Riga (1921), Treaty of Riga · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Riga was signed on 18 March 1921 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on one side and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on the other, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a>. It fixed a border that left western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and western Ukraine to Poland, and it remained in force until the Soviet invasion of Poland in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga.jpg" alt="Peace of Riga" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: official document of Poland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peace_treaty_of_Riga_1921.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> removed state borders across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> re-established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s independence after a century and a half of partition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> gave Poland, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, a chance to regain parts of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. In 1920 Piłsudski's offensive into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, meant to establish an independent Ukraine allied to Poland, was met by a Soviet counter-offensive into central Poland, which the Soviets saw as a land bridge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. The war culminated in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, and further setbacks persuaded Soviet Russia to negotiate.</p><p>Talks began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> on 17 August 1920 and moved to Riga, where they resumed on 21 September. The chief negotiators were Jan Dąbski for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Adolph Joffe for the Soviet side, and an armistice signed on 12 October took effect on 18 October. Soviet delegates offered substantial territorial concessions, but the Polish delegation, dominated by the National Democrats, preferred a border further west to keep non-Polish minorities below one third of the population. A parliamentary delegation rejected an offer that would have left Minsk on the Polish side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty has 26 articles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> recognised Soviet Ukraine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and withdrew recognition of the Ukrainian People's Republic, its only ally in the war, which meant giving up Piłsudski's federal programme. The border lay about 250 kilometres east of the Curzon Line and brought large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> into the Second Polish Republic. Russia and Soviet Ukraine abandoned claims to lands west of the line. Poland was to receive 30 million gold rubles and railway materials worth 29 million gold rubles, and Russia was to return works of art taken from Polish lands after 1772. Both sides renounced war compensation, and article 7 guaranteed minorities the use of their language and religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a>, who had backed the Curzon Line, were at first reluctant to recognise a treaty made without them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognised it in March 1923, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in April. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> it was criticised from the start; Piłsudski, who took part only as an observer, called it an act of cowardice, and on 15 May 1921 he apologised to Ukrainian soldiers at the internment camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>. The treaty contributed to the failure of his plan for a Polish-led federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. It lasted until the Soviet invasion of Poland in 1939, and a 1945 agreement redefined the borders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Riga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Red Army also reconquered Ukraine, the Caucasus and Central Asia, but its advance into Poland was stopped at the Battle of Warsaw in August 1920, and the <b>Treaty of Riga</b> of 1921 fixed the western border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Peace of Riga</b> the Red Army held most of Ukraine, western Ukraine going to Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Riga</b> in 1921 divided Belarusian lands between it and the Second Polish Republic, and in 1922 it became a founding member of the Soviet Union.</blockquote><a href="/state/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>Petliura allied with the Second Polish Republic in 1920, but the <b>Peace of Riga</b> in 1921 divided Ukraine between Poland and the Ukrainian SSR.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
